PR #2637  overall: potential_risk
Spec: Spurious smstateen bit-2 documentation for jvt CSR removed — software that trusted this phantom enable bit had no actual hardware trapping of lower-privilege JVT access, allowing unprivileged control-flow redirection.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:233-243  (global — SMSTATEEN0 bit macros)
      No SMSTATEEN0_JVT (bit 2) macro is defined. Bits 58–63 are defined for real hardware controls (AIA, HSENVCFG, SSTATEEN0). The absence of a bit-2 definition is correct — it prevents any code from attempting to use the phantom smstateen-JVT linkage. The risk is forward-looking: a developer adding Zcmt support would naturally insert SMSTATEEN0_JVT here alongside the other bit definitions, which would be incorrect.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu.c:564-572  kvm_riscv_vcpu_setup_config
      Configures cfg->hstateen0 with only real hardware bits: SMSTATEEN0_HSENVCFG (62), SMSTATEEN0_AIA_* (58–60), SMSTATEEN0_SSTATEEN0 (63). No phantom bit 2 is added. The hstateen0 mask is gated on riscv_has_extension_unlikely(RISCV_ISA_EXT_SMSTATEEN), which is appropriate. A developer following the pre-fix spec might add SMSTATEEN0_JVT here believing HS-mode can trap VS-mode JVT access — but the current code correctly does not.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu.c:599-622  kvm_arch_vcpu_load
      Writes cfg->hstateen0 to CSR_HSTATEEN0 via both nacl and native CSR paths. Since cfg->hstateen0 does not contain bit 2 (see setup_config above), no phantom bit is written to hardware. Both code paths are gated by riscv_has_extension_unlikely(RISCV_ISA_EXT_SMSTATEEN). The hardware CSR write is correct for the bits that are actually populated. If a future change added bit 2 to cfg->hstateen0, the hardware CSR write would silently ignore that bit — but that bug would be in setup_config, not here.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu.c:757-760  kvm_riscv_vcpu_swap_in_guest_state
      On guest entry, swaps CSR_SSTATEEN0 between host and guest contexts via csr_swap(). The swap is gated by hstateen0[SMSTATEEN0_SSTATEEN0], which is correct for enabling sstateen0 access from VS-mode. However, the full sstateen0 value from smcsr->sstateen0 is written unconditionally to the hardware CSR. If a guest (or VMM via SET_ONE_REG) set bit 2 in sstateen0, the kernel writes it to hardware. If hardware preserves but ignores bit 2 (reads back as written but has no trapping side-effect), the guest kernel would believe it has disabled VU-mode JVT access when it has not — a phantom isolation within the guest. The kernel applies no bit-level validation to sstateen0.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu.c:771-774  kvm_riscv_vcpu_swap_in_host_state
      On guest exit, reverses the sstateen0 swap: reads hardware CSR_SSTATEEN0 back into smcsr->sstateen0 and restores the host value. If the guest wrote sstateen0 bit 2 (believing it traps VU-mode cm.jt/cm.jalt), the kernel dutifully saves and restores that value with no hardware enforcement. The read-back path would reveal whether hardware hardwires bit 2 to 0 (in which case the phantom bit is self-correcting) or preserves it (in which case the guest's illusion persists). Same lack of bit-level validation as the entry path.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/kvm_host.h:170-174  kvm_vcpu_config
      hstateen0 is a u64 field in the per-VCPU config struct. It is neutral storage — the security property depends entirely on which bits are populated into it. Currently only real smstateen bits (58–63) are written here (see setup_config). No phantom bit 2 is inserted. The struct itself carries no per-bit semantics and is correctly typed to hold the full hstateen0 CSR width.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/kvm_host.h:176-178  kvm_vcpu_smstateen_csr
      Guest smstateen CSR context holds a single sstateen0 field (unsigned long). This is the backing store for the guest's sstateen0 value that gets context-switched to/from hardware. The struct carries no per-bit semantics — any bit written by the guest (including phantom bit 2) is stored verbatim and swapped to hardware. The implicit invariant is that every writable bit in sstateen0 has a corresponding hardware trapping effect. This invariant is not enforced: a guest (or VMM via SET_ONE_REG) can write bit 2, the struct stores it, and the swap paths propagate it to hardware with no trapping consequence.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/uapi/asm/kvm.h:100-103  (struct kvm_riscv_smstateen_csr — UAPI)
      Stable UAPI struct for KVM_GET/SET_ONE_REG on smstateen CSRs. Exposes sstateen0 as a raw unsigned long with no bit-level semantics documented in the ABI. This is the interface through which QEMU/VMMs would expose phantom bit-2 JVT control to the guest. A mistaken bit-2 definition in VMM userspace would be propagated through this ABI into the kernel and onto the hardware CSR. Because it is a stable UAPI, any bit-level validation added later would need to preserve backwards compatibility — making a fix harder once the phantom bit has been exposed through this surface.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_onereg.c:560-577  kvm_riscv_vcpu_smstateen_set_csr
      KVM_SET_ONE_REG path for smstateen CSRs. Validates that SMSTATEEN extension is available and reg_num is in bounds, then stores the value unconditionally: ((unsigned long *)csr)[reg_num] = reg_val. There is NO bit-level validation of reg_val. Userspace (e.g., QEMU) can write arbitrary bits into the guest's sstateen0, including bit 2. The value is then context-switched to hardware on guest entry. If a VMM following the pre-fix spec sets bit 2, the guest OS would read it back as set, believe it has disabled VU-mode JVT access, but hardware disregards it — phantom security control surface exposed to userspace with no kernel-side filtering.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_onereg.c:579-596  kvm_riscv_vcpu_smstateen_get_csr
      KVM_GET_ONE_REG path for smstateen CSRs. Reads back the full sstateen0 value: *out_val = ((unsigned long *)csr)[reg_num]. Returns the complete register value including any phantom bit 2 that was previously written via SET_ONE_REG. This leaks the phantom bit value to userspace, reinforcing the illusion that bit 2 has hardware effect. Userspace (QEMU) may propagate this false capability to the guest, and the guest kernel may rely on it for VU-mode isolation. The get path has the same lack of bit-level filtering as the set path.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/hwcap.h:89-93  (global — RISCV_ISA_EXT definitions)
      Defines Zc sub-extensions ZCA (80), ZCB (81), ZCD (82), ZCF (83), ZCMOP (84) but no ZCMT. The table jump extension (Zcmt) is not yet enumerated. This is correct — adding ZCMT here without corresponding smstateen-bit-2 awareness would create the semantic gap where the kernel recognizes Zcmt hardware but doesn't know bit 2 is a phantom control. The current absence of ZCMT prevents any code path from incorrectly keying Zcmt trapping to smstateen.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_onereg.c:247-249  kvm_riscv_vcpu_isa_disable_allowed
      SSAIA is the only extension keyed off Smstateen for disable decisions: returns true (disable allowed) only if RISCV_ISA_EXT_SMSTATEEN is available. Zc/Zcmt extensions are not listed in the smstateen-guarded cases — they fall through to earlier cases (like Zca/Zcb/Zcd/Zcf/Zcmop at lines 205–209) that return false (no disable mechanism). This correctly reflects that Zcmt has no architectural disable mechanism via smstateen. The code structure makes it clear where a developer might incorrectly add a Zcmt case keyed to Smstateen.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_onereg.c:205-209  kvm_riscv_vcpu_isa_enable_allowed
      Zca, Zcb, Zcd, Zcf, ZCMOP are all marked as having no disable mechanism (return false from the switch, meaning enable is always allowed — no restriction). Zcmt is not yet defined so it has no case here. If Zcmt were added, it would similarly have no smstateen-based disable, since the hardware does not gate JVT/table-jump on any smstateen bit. This function correctly does not claim smstateen controls any Zc extension.
