  [BUG] lib/sbi/sbi_hart.c:122-135  mstatus_init
      OpenSBI bulk-clears all sstateen0-3 and hstateen0-3 registers to 0 unconditionally, which silently clears bit 2 in sstateen2/hstateen2. The RISC-V spec once documented smstateen bit 2 as the gate for lower-privilege JVT CSR access and cm.jt/cm.jalt instructions, but later removed this linkage: JVT is actually gated by the Zcmt bit in menvcfg/senvcfg, not by stateen bit 2. Hardware never traps JVT access based on stateen bit 2. By zeroing sstateen2, OpenSBI makes a supervisor OS reading sstateen2=0 believe U-mode JVT access will trap — but hardware ignores this bit and U-mode retains full read/write access to JVT, enabling control-flow redirection via cm.jt/cm.jalt. This is a textbook phantom-hardware-isolation vulnerability. Contrast with mstateen0 (lines 90-116) which uses an explicit whitelist of known-good bits.
